# Audit log workflow

`site_audit_entries` (migration 062, shown newest first in `/admin#audit`) is the internal log of changes to the website. Every entry's wording is approved by the site owner before it is recorded.

For every change that affects what visitors or admins see (cards, glossary, UI copy, admin pages, data fixes):

1. **Propose the entry first.** Before writing any audit insert, show the user the proposed `title`, `description`, `area` (`cards` | `glossary` | `ui` | `admin` | `data`), `happened_at` date, and which cards (row numbers) and glossary term keys it touches. Use the `AskQuestion` tool or ask in plain text, and wait for approval or corrections.
2. **Write the wording for the owner, not for a reviewer.** Say what changed on the site in plain Hebrew or English, as the owner would describe it later. No file names, no implementation detail, no "fixed bug in X".
3. **Record it only after approval**, as an `INSERT INTO site_audit_entries (...) ... ON CONFLICT (entry_key) DO NOTHING` inside the numbered migration that makes the change (or a small dedicated migration when the change has no migration). `entry_key` is a lowercase slug such as `2026-10-16-glossary-day-treatment`. `card_keys` are `directory_card_edits.card_key` values (`row|||svc`); `term_keys` are keys of the `terms` object in `index.html`; `migration` is the migration's file name.
4. Register the migration in `scripts/database_updates_master.sql` as `[ ]`, per the directory-entries rule. Never mark it `[x]`.

If the user rejects or edits the wording, use their version verbatim.
